1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,041
Today your scripts talk to the cloud, with only the power they need.

2
00:00:04,591 --> 00:00:08,394
Cloud S D Ks let Python create and read cloud resources.

3
00:00:08,394 --> 00:00:12,129
One leaked key with too much power can cost real money.

4
00:00:12,129 --> 00:00:14,641
So we write least privilege policies,

5
00:00:14,641 --> 00:00:17,086
and we practise against a free mock,

6
00:00:17,086 --> 00:00:19,055
with no cloud account at all.

7
00:00:19,605 --> 00:00:22,891
Every demo is typed live on Ubuntu twenty-four oh four.

8
00:00:24,941 --> 00:00:27,081
Cloud code runs with an identity,

9
00:00:27,081 --> 00:00:29,287
and that identity has permissions.

10
00:00:29,287 --> 00:00:33,049
Least privilege means giving it only the actions it needs,

11
00:00:33,049 --> 00:00:35,060
on only the resources it needs,

12
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:36,162
and nothing more.

13
00:00:36,712 --> 00:00:38,876
A policy is a jay son document.

14
00:00:38,876 --> 00:00:40,900
Each statement has an effect,

15
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:42,156
a list of actions,

16
00:00:42,156 --> 00:00:44,389
and the resources they apply to.

17
00:00:44,389 --> 00:00:46,064
A star means everything,

18
00:00:46,064 --> 00:00:47,669
so a star in an action,

19
00:00:47,669 --> 00:00:48,855
or in a resource,

20
00:00:48,855 --> 00:00:49,832
is a red flag.

21
00:00:50,382 --> 00:00:53,480
An S D K lets your Python code call the cloud.

22
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:55,164
For AWS it is boto three.

23
00:00:55,164 --> 00:00:59,811
Boto three finds credentials in a fixed order: environment variables,

24
00:00:59,811 --> 00:01:01,562
then the credentials file,

25
00:01:01,562 --> 00:01:03,380
then a role on the machine.

26
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:05,670
So never write a key in your code,

27
00:01:05,670 --> 00:01:07,556
and never commit one to git.

28
00:01:08,106 --> 00:01:11,276
To test cloud code without a cloud account,

29
00:01:11,276 --> 00:01:12,160
we use moto,

30
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:13,192
a mock of AWS.

31
00:01:13,192 --> 00:01:15,035
Your code runs unchanged,

32
00:01:15,035 --> 00:01:17,689
against a fake that lives in memory.

33
00:01:17,689 --> 00:01:19,532
That keeps practice free,

34
00:01:19,532 --> 00:01:19,901
safe,

35
00:01:19,901 --> 00:01:20,564
and fast.

36
00:01:21,114 --> 00:01:24,660
If you ever use a real account: free tier resources only,

37
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:26,216
least privilege policies,

38
00:01:26,216 --> 00:01:27,460
and when you finish,

39
00:01:27,460 --> 00:01:29,326
delete everything you created,

40
00:01:29,326 --> 00:01:31,504
so nothing keeps costing you money.

41
00:01:33,554 --> 00:01:39,442
First, a policy that follows least privilege. Show it. Type: cat, policy dot jay son.

42
00:01:40,292 --> 00:01:41,375
Two statements.

43
00:01:41,375 --> 00:01:43,902
The first lets you list one bucket.

44
00:01:43,902 --> 00:01:46,213
The second lets you get objects,

45
00:01:46,213 --> 00:01:47,946
only inside that bucket.

46
00:01:47,946 --> 00:01:49,245
No stars anywhere.

47
00:01:49,245 --> 00:01:52,206
Now an audit script that flags wildcards.

48
00:01:52,206 --> 00:01:52,711
Run it.

49
00:01:52,711 --> 00:01:54,877
Type: dot slash audit dot P Y,

50
00:01:54,877 --> 00:01:56,249
policy dot jay son.

51
00:01:57,299 --> 00:01:57,690
Okay,

52
00:01:57,690 --> 00:02:00,037
no wildcards: least privilege.

53
00:02:00,037 --> 00:02:01,367
Now a bad policy,

54
00:02:01,367 --> 00:02:04,261
that allows everything on everything.

55
00:02:04,261 --> 00:02:06,608
Type: dot slash audit dot P Y,

56
00:02:06,608 --> 00:02:07,859
bad dot jay son.

57
00:02:08,909 --> 00:02:11,643
Two problems found: the action is a star,

58
00:02:11,643 --> 00:02:13,444
and the resource is a star.

59
00:02:13,444 --> 00:02:16,177
That policy hands over the whole account.

60
00:02:16,177 --> 00:02:19,511
Anyone holding those keys could delete everything.

61
00:02:19,511 --> 00:02:21,112
Now check the exit code.

62
00:02:21,112 --> 00:02:21,845
Type: echo,

63
00:02:21,845 --> 00:02:22,312
dollar,

64
00:02:22,312 --> 00:02:23,245
question mark.

65
00:02:24,295 --> 00:02:24,545
One.

66
00:02:24,545 --> 00:02:26,727
The audit fails with exit code one,

67
00:02:26,727 --> 00:02:29,906
so a pipeline can block the change before it ships.

68
00:02:31,956 --> 00:02:33,546
Now the S D K itself.

69
00:02:33,546 --> 00:02:37,333
Boto three is the official Python library for AWS.

70
00:02:37,333 --> 00:02:39,378
Moto is a free mock of AWS,

71
00:02:39,378 --> 00:02:41,726
running inside your own script.

72
00:02:41,726 --> 00:02:43,923
Nothing leaves your computer,

73
00:02:43,923 --> 00:02:45,740
and nothing costs money.

74
00:02:45,740 --> 00:02:46,952
Show the script.

75
00:02:46,952 --> 00:02:47,710
Type: cat,

76
00:02:47,710 --> 00:02:49,300
S three list dot P Y.

77
00:02:49,850 --> 00:02:50,879
The decorator,

78
00:02:50,879 --> 00:02:51,541
mock AWS,

79
00:02:51,541 --> 00:02:54,482
sends every boto three call to the fake.

80
00:02:54,482 --> 00:02:57,276
Notice what is missing: no access key,

81
00:02:57,276 --> 00:02:59,335
and no password in the code.

82
00:02:59,335 --> 00:03:02,202
Boto three finds credentials by itself.

83
00:03:02,202 --> 00:03:02,717
Run it.

84
00:03:02,717 --> 00:03:04,114
Type: python three,

85
00:03:04,114 --> 00:03:05,658
S three list dot P Y.

86
00:03:07,208 --> 00:03:08,158
Three objects,

87
00:03:08,158 --> 00:03:09,311
with their sizes,

88
00:03:09,311 --> 00:03:12,093
from a bucket that exists only in memory.

89
00:03:12,093 --> 00:03:13,857
Real calls fail sometimes,

90
00:03:13,857 --> 00:03:15,214
so handle the error.

91
00:03:15,214 --> 00:03:16,774
Show the second script.

92
00:03:16,774 --> 00:03:17,453
Type: cat,

93
00:03:17,453 --> 00:03:18,877
S three miss dot P Y.

94
00:03:19,427 --> 00:03:22,104
We ask for a bucket that does not exist,

95
00:03:22,104 --> 00:03:23,643
and catch client error.

96
00:03:23,643 --> 00:03:26,053
The error code says what went wrong.

97
00:03:26,053 --> 00:03:26,521
Run it.

98
00:03:26,521 --> 00:03:27,793
Type: python three,

99
00:03:27,793 --> 00:03:29,198
S three miss dot P Y.

100
00:03:30,548 --> 00:03:32,032
Caught: no such bucket.

101
00:03:32,032 --> 00:03:35,516
With a real account you would also meet access denied,

102
00:03:35,516 --> 00:03:38,355
and that means your policy is doing its job.

103
00:03:38,355 --> 00:03:39,387
Catch the error,

104
00:03:39,387 --> 00:03:39,838
log it,

105
00:03:39,838 --> 00:03:41,193
and exit with a code.

106
00:03:43,243 --> 00:03:44,328
Here is your task,

107
00:03:44,328 --> 00:03:45,836
with everything you need.

108
00:03:45,836 --> 00:03:47,765
The files are in the repository,

109
00:03:47,765 --> 00:03:49,152
and in the description.

110
00:03:49,702 --> 00:03:55,377
The repository is git hub dot com, slash it dash waves, slash dev ops dash A I.

111
00:03:55,927 --> 00:04:00,608
In the academy slash lesson fifty-four folder you will find the task file,

112
00:04:00,608 --> 00:04:01,620
and the checker,

113
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:03,202
check fifty-four dot S H.

114
00:04:03,752 --> 00:04:05,132
You write two files.

115
00:04:05,132 --> 00:04:05,545
First,

116
00:04:05,545 --> 00:04:09,064
policy dot jay son: read-only access to one bucket,

117
00:04:09,064 --> 00:04:10,927
with no star in any action,

118
00:04:10,927 --> 00:04:13,480
and only that bucket as the resource.

119
00:04:14,030 --> 00:04:14,510
Second,

120
00:04:14,510 --> 00:04:16,018
S three tools dot P Y,

121
00:04:16,018 --> 00:04:17,115
with a function,

122
00:04:17,115 --> 00:04:17,801
big files.

123
00:04:17,801 --> 00:04:19,035
It takes a client,

124
00:04:19,035 --> 00:04:20,955
a bucket and a minimum size,

125
00:04:20,955 --> 00:04:24,110
and returns the sorted keys at least that big.

126
00:04:24,110 --> 00:04:27,127
It must handle more than a thousand objects,

127
00:04:27,127 --> 00:04:28,430
so use a paginator,

128
00:04:28,430 --> 00:04:31,310
and an empty bucket returns an empty list.

129
00:04:31,860 --> 00:04:33,221
The checker uses moto,

130
00:04:33,221 --> 00:04:35,015
so you need no cloud account.

131
00:04:35,015 --> 00:04:38,665
Do not open the solution files until you have really tried.

132
00:04:39,215 --> 00:04:41,060
The checker shows six pass lines.

133
00:04:41,060 --> 00:04:42,794
Write your results in a report,

134
00:04:42,794 --> 00:04:44,527
and end it with a cleanup line.

135
00:04:46,577 --> 00:04:50,502
Here is how your work is judged. Six points, and you need all six.

136
00:04:51,052 --> 00:04:52,887
The checker shows six pass lines.

137
00:04:53,437 --> 00:04:56,658
The policy is read-only, with no star in any action.

138
00:04:57,208 --> 00:05:00,494
The resources name only the one bucket, and its objects.

139
00:05:01,044 --> 00:05:04,969
Big files handles more than a thousand objects, and an empty bucket.

140
00:05:05,519 --> 00:05:09,039
No keys in your code, and the report ends with a cleanup line.

141
00:05:09,589 --> 00:05:13,002
The report is complete, and you worked in your own virtual machine.

